I regarded my visitor as an impertinent simpleton; but I have been amazed, in subsequent years, at finding that her creed is that of hundreds of housewives more or less sensible. &#34;Luck&#34; rules the baking, and upon the shoulders of this Invisible are laid the deficiencies of the complacent cook. Cheap flour and laziness are at the bottom of more mishaps in the bread line than any other combination of circumstances. From the inferior grades of flour, it is possible to make tolerable biscuit, crumpets, and muffins, plain pastry, and very good griddle-cakes. You cannot, by any stress of art, produce excellent bread from poor flour. It is no economy to purchase it for this purpose. It 
 is judicious to lay in two barrels at a time, and to use the best only for the semi- or tri-weekly baking.

